WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:04.700
One franchise that's been the butt of Rick
and Morty jokes time and time again has been

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:05.320
Marvel.

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:10.320
From Hulk references to Avengers jokes, here’s
all the times Rick and Morty poked fun at

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:10.900
Marvel.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:15.820
In the season two episode "Total Rickall,"
the Smith family finds themselves plagued

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:20.650
by an interstellar parasite that has the ability
to implant false memories into its hosts.

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:25.300
Each time they have a flashback, a new parasite
joins their midst in the form of a wacky character.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:25.960
"Wait.

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:28.800
Don't leave without letting Photographer Raptor
take a picture."

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:36.400
In one such dubious memory, the family, along
with their friend Mr. Poopybutthole, is seen

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.140
leaving a musical play based on the life of
the Hulk.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:43.760
They seem pretty excited about the play, and
Jerry is even wearing a pair of fake Hulk

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:44.260
hands.

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:48.179
This is actually a two-for-one reference that
not only gives a nod to the big green guy

00:00:48.179 --> 00:00:52.940
but also seems to be an allusion to the real,
disastrous Spider-Man Broadway musical that

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:56.340
injured several stunt performers starting
back in 2010.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:00.680
A number of these injuries were life-threatening,
and the play experienced a range of other

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:01.480
calamities.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:07.320
With a price tag of $75 million dollars, Spider-Man:
Turn Off the Dark is the most expensive Broadway

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:11.910
musical ever made, and even though it holds
the Broadway box office record for single-week

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:17.470
sales, taking in $2.9 million over a span
of nine performances, it still lost nearly

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:18.820
$60 million.

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:23.860
In a show with as rabid a fanbase as Rick
and Morty, it's hard to pick any single episode

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:28.540
as a "fan favorite," but two that stand out
are the Interdimensional Cable anthologies.

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:33.540
In the second installment, we're briefly introduced
to Octopus-Man as he somewhat nonsensically

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:37.780
gets into a car with a mysterious driver bearing
a scar across his face.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:41.740
Claiming to be a marine biologist who was
bitten by an octopus which thereby turned

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:46.400
him into a half-human, half-octopus Octopus-Man
is obviously a riff on Spider-Man.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:51.740
But while Spidey is a good guy, Octopus-Man
claims to "save people," which basically translates

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:54.020
to him stabbing random people on the street.

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:57.760
So maybe he's really more akin to Spider-Man's
nemesis, Dr. Octopus?

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:02.130
Or maybe in an episode as openly nutty as
this particular one, it's best not to dig

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:03.200
too deep for logic.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:07.480
"Well Summer, maybe people that create things
aren't concerned with your delicate sensibilities,

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:07.980
you know?"

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:12.280
When Rick and Morty find themselves on the
Purge Planet in season two's "Look Who's Purging

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:15.380
Now," a moment comes when it seems like they're
cornered once and for all.

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:19.440
Then Rick's earlier message to Summer pays
off, and suddenly, Rick and Morty are decked

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:24.600
out in a pair of battle suits that seem suspiciously
similar to those worn by Iron Man and War

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:25.220
Machine.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:29.140
Not only do these suits come packed with a
seemingly endless series of gadgets used for

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:33.670
attacking an adversary in every way imaginable,
and not only are their wearers practically

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:37.980
invincible, but even the delivery method is
strikingly similar to something Tony Stark

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:38.760
would have designed.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:46.120
"Morty, time to purge."

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:52.180
The real kicker is the song that plays when
the suits are first activated "Feels Good"

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:53.460
by the R&amp;B group Tony!

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:53.960
Toni!

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:54.460
Toné!

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:57.700
If that's not a sneaky Tony Stark reference,
we don't know what is.

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:02.620
In the show's third season, we're introduced
to the Vindicators, a team of oddball superheroes

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:05.980
that are about as obvious a knockoff of the
Avengers as it gets.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:06.980
"Rick.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:08.480
Is that the Vindibeacon?

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:10.540
We're being called to assemble by the Vindicators!"

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:14.080
"I refuse to answer a literal call to adventure,
Morty, let it go to voicemail."

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:18.220
First of all, there's the name, as "vindicate"
and "avenge" are basically synonyms.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:22.120
Then there are the two groups' symbols, because
when you turn the Vindicators' symbol upside

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:25.340
down, it looks suspiciously similar to that
of the Avengers.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:28.760
The story of the Vindicators is in itself
extremely Avengery.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:33.360
We meet the team in "Vindicators 3: The Return
of Worldender," in which it's made clear that

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:37.100
there was a first Vindicator team-up that
included Rick and Morty, as well as a second

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:39.260
one they skipped for reasons that remain vague.

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:43.860
"This article says the reason we weren't involved
was 'personality conflicts.'"

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:47.960
Could this be a reference to the second Avengers
film, Age of Ultron, which many agree was

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:49.880
one of the lesser MCU installments?

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:54.400
At one point, Rick also explicitly refers
to Morty's Vindicators "phase," and as we

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.220
all know, the MCU releases are broken up into
a series of phases.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:02.980
As the episode "Vindicators 3: The Return
of Worldender" makes blatantly clear, said

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:07.260
superhero group has assembled to do battle
against a being called Worldender.

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:11.160
Little is known about the villain, other than
the fact that he possesses great power and

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:12.340
a taste for genocide.

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:17.400
"Vindicators, Worldender is back, and this
time, he's out to end more than worlds."

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:19.040
Sound like anyone familiar?

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:23.700
This is almost certainly a teasing rip-off
of Thanos or Galactus or maybe even both.

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:28.110
He's the cliché mega-villain who's supposedly
unstoppable unless facing the combined might

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:30.060
of the uh, the Vindicators.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:34.240
His on-the-nose name also seems to point out
how tired audiences are becoming of these

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:35.520
over-the-top baddies.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:39.960
If Worldender is supposed to be some kind
of Thanos-style enemy who verges on invulnerability,

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:44.060
Rick and Morty sticks its tongue out at this
concept once again by revealing that Rick

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:47.220
has effortlessly defeated him off-screen while
blackout drunk.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:50.840
"No offense, but I've always suspected that
a lot of what you do in a year could be knocked

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:52.020
out in a couple of hours."

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:56.539
Among the most popular of the MCU's leading
characters is Star-Lord, leader of the Guardians

00:04:56.540 --> 00:04:57.420
of the Galaxy.

00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:02.099
Played by one of the most beloved superstars
in Hollywood, Star-Lord is known for his charm,

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:03.880
good looks, humor, and bravery.

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:08.189
His courage has been instrumental in saving
the galaxy on multiple occasions, even if

00:05:08.189 --> 00:05:12.490
his lack of self-control did directly result
in the Thanos snap, which erased half of all

00:05:12.490 --> 00:05:13.780
the life in the universe.

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:15.360
But that's a different conversation.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:19.839
In any case, Star-Lord got the Rick and Morty
treatment in "Vindicators 3: The Return of

00:05:19.839 --> 00:05:24.740
Worldender" with the appearance of Vindicators
member Vance Maximus, Renegade Starsoldier,

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:26.660
voiced by the hilarious Christian Slater.

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:31.280
Like Star-Lord, he flies around with a jetpack,
wields some kind of ray gun, and seems to

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:33.000
offer plenty of swagger and wit.

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:33.880
"Sorry I'm late.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:35.040
It was happy hour."

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:39.780
However, unlike Star-Lord who spends much
of his screen-time acting in his own self

00:05:39.789 --> 00:05:44.289
interest before coming through as the hero
in the end Maximus does the opposite, selfishly

00:05:44.289 --> 00:05:48.200
attempting to save his own skin before getting
brutally slain for his efforts.

